# Combat-PoD Duel Builder Guide

This guide covers how to start a combat-minigame fight from script --
the create / add / finalize builder.

For the system internals (variable namespace, slot lifecycle, execution
phases, result codes), see `COMBAT_REFERENCE.info`. This guide focuses on
the authoring workflow: how to assemble a roster and kick off a fight.

The builder effects live in
`devpod/common/scripted_effects/POD_combat/pod_combat_group_effects.txt`.

---

## Do you even need the builder?

Most duels should NOT call the builder directly.

PoD's whole duel system (`hold_court`, schemes, activity events, the
`single_combat.*` chain, etc.) routes through
`configure_start_single_combat_effect`. The combat minigame overrides that
effect (in `PoD_override_single_combat_effects.txt`) and builds a **1v1**
for you -- shell event + create + add the opponent + finalize, with the
full caller context (follow-up event, your saved scopes, rule flags)
preserved.

**Interception is conditional.** The override intercepts only when ALL of
these hold; otherwise the call falls through to PoD's original duel body
(Part 4 of the override) and no minigame runs:

| Gate | Falls through when |
|---|---|
| `has_game_rule = pod_combat_enabled` | the player turned the combat rule off |
| Exactly one of `SC_ATTACKER` / `SC_DEFENDER` is `is_ai = no` | PC-vs-PC (the UI is single-perspective) or NPC-vs-NPC |
| `SC_ATTACKER != SC_DEFENDER` | a self-match -- Part 2 fires `INVALIDATION_EVENT` instead, and it never reaches either body |
| `FIXED` is neither `sc_attacker` nor `sc_defender` | the story predetermines a winner, which vanilla honors correctly and the minigame does not implement |
| Neither principal has `pod_combat_active` | one of them is already hosting a live fight |

So if you're writing a normal story duel, just call
`configure_start_single_combat_effect` the way PoD always has, and accept
that an NPC-vs-NPC or rule-disabled instance resolves through vanilla.

**Call the builder directly only when you want a custom roster** the
single-combat path can't express -- group fights, hand-picked combatants,
ally/enemy line-ups that aren't "PC vs one opponent." The debug
interactions in `POD_debug_interactions.txt` are the worked reference; the
1vN / Nv1 / NvN ones build their rosters by hand.

### Standard launcher vs raw builder

The two routes are not interchangeable. A direct builder call bypasses the
duel shell, so it loses caller context and some normal duel bookkeeping:

| | `configure_start_single_combat_effect` | raw `POD_combat_create_duel_effect` |
|---|---|---|
| Roster | 1v1 only (PC vs one opponent) | anything you seat |
| Interception gates | applied (table above) | none -- you are past them |
| Falls back to vanilla duel | yes, when a gate fails | no -- nothing runs if your roster is bad |
| Duel shell (`pod_combat.9995`) | opened before the build | not opened |
| Your parent saved scopes in the follow-up | preserved intact | **lost** (principals + rule flags only) |
| `INVALIDATION_EVENT` | fired on a self-match | not a parameter, never fired |
| `engaged_in_single_combat` set on both principals | yes | no (only released at end) |
| `remove_single_combat_info_effect`, shirtlessness / signature-weapon setup | yes | no |
| Terrain background captured at setup | yes | reconstructed at end, degraded |
| Ends via | the shell card's Conclude option | the combat window's End Combat button |

There is currently **no public launcher that combines a custom starting
roster with the shell's parent-scope preservation**. If your follow-up
needs parent scopes, a 1v1 through the standard launcher is the only
supported route; a custom roster means writing the follow-up against the
principals alone.

